  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes experimental routes for generating human adipose models from stem cells or native fat tissue. In the top half, human pluripotent stem cells are directed toward adipocytes through several differentiation strategies. One path forms embryoid bodies, then mesenchymal progenitor-like cells, which can become either white or brown adipocytes depending on added regulatory factors. Other routes use spheroids or mesoderm induction to bias cells more specifically toward brown adipocytes, with suspension culture followed by adherent maturation. Overall, the schematic emphasizes that culture format and signaling cues determine whether the resulting fat cells are white, brown, or more lineage-restricted intermediates.\n\nThe lower half shows how adipose tissue can be turned into increasingly complex in vitro models. Starting from adipose tissue, stromal vascular fraction cells are isolated and used in methods such as hanging-drop culture, low-attachment culture, or embedding in matrix. These approaches produce adipose spheroids, vascularized adipose organoids, or immune cell–containing organoids. The diagram also highlights broader bioengineering platforms, including tissue engineering, 3D bioprinting, and organ-on-chip systems. Taken together, the figure presents a toolkit for building adipose organoids with varying levels of cellular complexity and physiological realism.",
